GStreamer (codec-gst crate, H.264 codec)
----------------------------------------
Runtime components loaded dynamically from the user's system (never bundled
or statically linked by this project):

| Component                  | License           | Notes                                        |
|----------------------------|-------------------|----------------------------------------------|
| GStreamer runtime          | LGPL-2.1-or-later | Linux: system package. Windows: official runtime installed by Setup. Not redistributed |
| gst-libav (avdec_h264)     | LGPL              | libav/FFmpeg plugin; loaded dynamically; Linux and Windows |
| gst-plugins-ugly (x264enc) | GPL-2.0-or-later  | x264 encoder (gstx264 on Windows); loaded dynamically; Linux and Windows hosts |
| PipeWire                   | MIT               | Optional; Linux Wayland capture only         |

- The gstreamer-rs Rust bindings are licensed MIT/Apache-2.0.
- The GStreamer runtime libraries are licensed LGPL-2.1-or-later.
- The avdec_h264 decoder (libav/gst-libav) is LGPL, like the rest of the
  runtime; it is loaded dynamically from the system and is not bundled.
- The x264enc element (gst-plugins-ugly, libx264) is licensed GPL.
- Trinix loads these libraries dynamically from the system at run
  time and never bundles or redistributes them.
- Bundling GStreamer or any plugin with a release needs a separate license
  review first.
- Windows: Setup downloads the official GStreamer runtime (MSVC build, pinned
  version, SHA-256 verified) from the GStreamer project at install time and
  installs it under the current user's local application folder. Setup checks
  for gstreamer-1.0-0.dll, gstlibav.dll and gstx264.dll. The Windows host
  encodes with x264enc and the viewer decodes with avdec_h264. These
  components are installed and used at run time but are not included in
  Trinix's installer, binaries or source, and are not statically linked.
- Android uses the platform MediaCodec API and has no GStreamer dependency.

Desktop viewer (egui/eframe)
--------------------------
- eframe/egui 0.31.1: MIT OR Apache-2.0, desktop UI and OpenGL textures.
  https://github.com/emilk/egui
- async-trait: MIT OR Apache-2.0, connector implementation; Tokio (MIT) reused.
- New GUI transitive packages use permissive MIT/Apache/BSD/ISC/Zlib,
  Unicode-3.0, BSL-1.0, CC0-1.0, OFL-1.1/Ubuntu-font-1.0 (fonts),
  or other permissive alternatives recorded in
  Cargo.lock and each package's license metadata. No GUI GPL/LGPL code is bundled.
  GStreamer system runtime licensing remains as described above.


Windows desktop capture uses the LGPL-licensed GStreamer d3d11 plugin
(d3d11screencapturesrc, built on Windows DXGI) through its public pipeline API.
The Rust adapter is original.
GStreamer Windows SDK distribution, runtime DLLs and plugins retain their
individual licenses; obtain them from the official GStreamer project. The
pinned CI installer checksum is from the vendor's published .sha256sum file.

Secure connections use Rustls (Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT), ring (ISC-style and
permissive notices), rcgen (MIT OR Apache-2.0), SHA-2/base64/subtle/zeroize and
getrandom (permissive licenses recorded in Cargo.lock/package metadata). TLS is
used through their public APIs; certificate and viewing-token integration is original.

Android JNI integration uses jni 0.21.1 (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and its permissively
licensed dependencies. Android UI/MediaCodec glue and Rust networking code are
original. AndroidX test libraries are used only for emulator instrumentation.

Desktop connection QR codes use qrcode 0.14.1 (MIT OR Apache-2.0), without its
optional image/rendering dependencies. The app paints the module matrix directly.

Desktop remote input uses Enigo 0.6.1 (MIT), through its public keyboard/mouse
API on Windows and X11. Wayland input uses the existing ashpd dependency and
the system RemoteDesktop portal. Android accessibility/IME integration is original.
https://github.com/enigo-rs/enigo

LAN discovery library
---------------------
- mdns-sd 0.13.11 (Apache-2.0 OR MIT), used through its public DNS-SD API
  with default features disabled: https://github.com/keepsimple1/mdns-sd
- Notable transitive crates: flume (Apache-2.0 OR MIT), socket2
  (Apache-2.0 OR MIT), mio (MIT), if-addrs (MIT OR BSD-3-Clause),
  spin (MIT), fastrand (Apache-2.0 OR MIT), log (Apache-2.0 OR MIT). Exact resolved versions
  and platform dependencies are recorded in Cargo.lock. The discovery adapter
  and TXT schema are original Trinix code.

Discovery directly uses if-addrs 0.13.4 (MIT OR BSD-3-Clause), already used
by mdns-sd, to enumerate local interface addresses and subnet prefixes.
Source: https://github.com/messense/if-addrs
